meta {
  name: Verify Domain
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/agency/domains/{{customDomainId}}/verify
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("fails for a domain whose records do not exist", function () {
    // `example.com` publishes no `_sfa-verify` TXT record for us, so this is the
    // expected outcome in the collection. A `failed` result is data, not an
    // error — the owner needs to see why.
    expect(res.getBody().status).to.equal("failed");
    expect(res.getBody().lastError).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
  });

  test("records when it was checked", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().lastCheckedAt).to.be.a("string");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /agency/domains/:domainId/verify

  Re-check a custom domain's DNS and activate it if ownership is proven.

  **Module** — none. **Permission** — `agency:domains:write`.

  Idempotent and safe to hammer: it is a read of public DNS, and an owner
  watching for propagation will press it repeatedly. An already-`active` domain
  is returned unchanged rather than erroring.

  ## What counts as proof
  A `TXT` record at `_sfa-verify.<domain>` matching the row's token. Control of
  a subdomain of the zone is control of the zone — the standard every ACME and
  SaaS custom-domain flow uses.

  **Pointing DNS at us is checked but is *not* proof.** Anyone can CNAME their
  own domain at our IP; that says nothing about who owns the name. So a domain
  whose TXT record is correct is activated **even if the CNAME/A record has not
  propagated yet**, with the routing problem reported in `lastError` — the DNS
  propagation the owner is waiting on is the same propagation that would fail
  that check.

  **Response `200`** — the domain row. `status` becomes `active` (with
  `verifiedAt` set) or `failed` (with `lastError` explaining what to fix).
  A `failed` verification is **not** an HTTP error.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — the domain needs no verification (it is a platform subdomain).
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:domains:write`.
  - `404` — no such domain **in this agency**. Scoped in the query, so another
    tenant's domain is indistinguishable from one that does not exist.
}
